if you drive a car long enough odds are  you'll end up in some sort of fender  bender which will  lead to questions about who pays for the  damages that's the topic of today's  conversation with eric palacios  in our las vegas now segment so in the  event of an accident how do you know  whether  you're going to have to pay for it your  insurance company is going to have to  pay for it or maybe somebody else's  insurance company will pay for it well  the first question is who is at fault  for the accident  if it's your fault and you have full  coverage  then your insurance company will cover  the damages  to repair your car or the full value of  it if it's  deemed a total loss you do have a  deductible however and that varies uh  according to the policy normally between  250 it could be all the way up to a  thousand dollars  the danger though for people who just  adhere to the law  enough and only have liability coverage  is if  they're in an accident and it is their  own fault they're on their own  that's true you're on your own if you  just have liability  and you cause the accident if you're  responsible for it  or the person that causes the accident  if it's somebody else's fault if they  don't have insurance  uh then usually it's on your own dime to  get the car repaired  what recourse do you have if the other  person doesn't have insurance  well if you have a full coverage then  your own insurance company  uh will step in and repair your vehicle  sometimes uh it's not clear who's at  fault and insurance companies take  days or weeks to decide uh who is at  fault and if they're going to step in  and repair it  if you can't wait for that decision to  be made even if the accident wasn't your  fault  if you have full coverage you can have  your own insurance company  repair the vehicle you will have to pay  the deductible up front  but if it's deemed to be the other  person's fault then you can get that  deductible back  insurance companies are famous for  dragging their feet when it comes to  paying for repairs because there's the  back and forth that goes on  what do people do when that happens well  you can have your own insurance company  step in and take care of it  that usually makes it go a little bit  faster if it's not your fault  uh you don't have to worry your rates  won't go up uh your insurance company  will  get the money from the insurance company  of the at fault  driver a lot of people are very hesitant  to use their own insurance  thinking that it's going to make their  premiums increase but if the accident  isn't your fault  if you're just making a claim because  there was damage uh  caused by somebody else then your  insurance will not go up  what if you feel your own insurance  company is dragging their feet and  and not paying what you feel they should  be paying for  there's different things you can do one  you can talk to an attorney uh two  if it's not to the level where you need  to involve an attorney you can write  a complaint to the nevada division of  insurance they'll investigate the matter  so there are some things that you can do  to protect yourself and get the  insurance company to act a little faster  bottom line you find people are much  better off when they are fully insured  it's always a good idea especially with  the the economy the way it is we find  a lot of drivers are out there without a  current insurance they don't pay their  premium so it's cancelled  and then uh if you are hit by one of  these drivers  you're on your own if you don't have not  just full coverage to cover your car  but also um uim which is uninsured or  underinsured motorist coverage to  protect you  in the event during an accident all  right eric always good information  thanks so much for joining us  thanks for having me you can give eric  palacios and associates a call at that  number 702-444-7777
 

